[{:db/id "q-339746/field-1/0ab5179a643a123ebc5759e29002b95a10fa75402ca63a1ff627d27904361aec" :answer/id #uuid "6d7ab00d-cd37-515a-beb9-f62ff448e618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339746/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" :answer/id #uuid "de09f742-6633-5580-9dcc-5cd239ed6a45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339746/field-1/91afb9a0ba928957f30252e15bfbaf7fa3834bb4fbb0b0864156247fdeeccc36" :answer/id #uuid "45199a01-42f8-5382-9e98-b7738bca85e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.03" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339746/field-1/a9aa1e28dfb18079fa45e44d11e7788d19ea22e96356b46fb7dbbea6bc2e7d49" :answer/id #uuid "78353621-d875-57df-8dd0-fbb367e2acde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.31" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339746/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "58dcd14e-7b03-5264-8d1d-f06032a3fa02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339746/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" :answer-field/choices ["q-339746/field-1/0ab5179a643a123ebc5759e29002b95a10fa75402ca63a1ff627d27904361aec" "q-339746/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" "q-339746/field-1/91afb9a0ba928957f30252e15bfbaf7fa3834bb4fbb0b0864156247fdeeccc36" "q-339746/field-1/a9aa1e28dfb18079fa45e44d11e7788d19ea22e96356b46fb7dbbea6bc2e7d49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339746" :question/id #uuid "eb5c3ca0-3507-57c3-ab49-eeb884b54da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-339746" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.00.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339746/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339746"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755/field-1/7c2fec16fd7ae02af1a8818a9096a676afc42249e7e68d108c3f64859048a45f" :answer/id #uuid "0f7b4238-e778-5aa0-a054-d6a92be8ca59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755/field-1/2292b9d44ea614576b621c3adbda61cd7c9e4e41ac69460ccdc207757d1f004e" :answer/id #uuid "c447796c-92fd-50a7-8b00-8e9920f7e84a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755/field-1/0c5516bd846cd9f8dc992ee0d8a81ab0c5e379b8d42743c1e6578f894c5f2a5b" :answer/id #uuid "d1c04133-a9b8-5fbb-936f-e68c27ded630" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755/field-1/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" :answer/id #uuid "5a61fa4b-efcd-5c99-80f7-e0bc43b9cd89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a633da-d67c-5ca9-bfe5-7e30dd02a524" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339755/field-1/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" :answer-field/choices ["q-339755/field-1/7c2fec16fd7ae02af1a8818a9096a676afc42249e7e68d108c3f64859048a45f" "q-339755/field-1/2292b9d44ea614576b621c3adbda61cd7c9e4e41ac69460ccdc207757d1f004e" "q-339755/field-1/0c5516bd846cd9f8dc992ee0d8a81ab0c5e379b8d42743c1e6578f894c5f2a5b" "q-339755/field-1/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339755" :question/id #uuid "5bf7de1b-3e49-5cb2-9cc7-c1247a864f21" :question/math-academy-id "q-339755" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3}),$ rounded to two decimal places\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{3}-0=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+2y+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈0.25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339755/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339755"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-1/6c43fc7615f77c3fc4c8cf67229e4eec1a3a8bb862cd2a795ae1f3b47d1c472d" :answer/id #uuid "9fdaecd3-6323-5972-8194-aa71d7243ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-1/a3010a177c248be854375bd28fb35f06e72b32ddfdbcd52d1055c93b49d20aeb" :answer/id #uuid "9e1fa2ae-92a9-541e-85e9-566450abe17c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-1/fc3b63992da517118e36ed60a8a02b583ae43a0c8efce461bba7c3b204a7f3dc" :answer/id #uuid "dbe9e337-0273-5427-8669-36ffc1e38b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e29467-09a8-5fba-9e0b-2488a2dfdf74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339865/field-1/fc3b63992da517118e36ed60a8a02b583ae43a0c8efce461bba7c3b204a7f3dc" :answer-field/choices ["q-339865/field-1/6c43fc7615f77c3fc4c8cf67229e4eec1a3a8bb862cd2a795ae1f3b47d1c472d" "q-339865/field-1/a3010a177c248be854375bd28fb35f06e72b32ddfdbcd52d1055c93b49d20aeb" "q-339865/field-1/fc3b63992da517118e36ed60a8a02b583ae43a0c8efce461bba7c3b204a7f3dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-2/652cf1aaa239ada7f241558c91a491bc28687af266bc94dfedaa05c9892ebeec" :answer/id #uuid "d3a5e5f4-538a-51e6-8ee7-35dc1f9d0f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10.59" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-2/511a01a3c633d52235180c6839d80d1971c1bafa3264c160f35755e3b7d3a795" :answer/id #uuid "bb65152d-0d49-5daf-9f1f-2ddcc1a516f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.93" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-2/45f267707e715d751ad23b22ac13c10e0e3f4465423b6ad533b3c263fc233904" :answer/id #uuid "fe15311d-f066-5afe-875c-25cb3195dfaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb612ac-0689-5478-9a28-3a77e717a50f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339865/field-2/45f267707e715d751ad23b22ac13c10e0e3f4465423b6ad533b3c263fc233904" :answer-field/choices ["q-339865/field-2/652cf1aaa239ada7f241558c91a491bc28687af266bc94dfedaa05c9892ebeec" "q-339865/field-2/511a01a3c633d52235180c6839d80d1971c1bafa3264c160f35755e3b7d3a795" "q-339865/field-2/45f267707e715d751ad23b22ac13c10e0e3f4465423b6ad533b3c263fc233904"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339865" :question/id #uuid "4a6c86e8-ee41-5d34-a9e5-2474fc7e8afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-339865" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=3.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+6\\cdot (3.25)) \\\\  & =10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.25,\\,y(1)≈10.50.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339865/field-1" "q-339865/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339865"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-1/9249ec38b82e800720a79ba1072f7d45fcdfddce5a4914d085b8568b9b083f3a" :answer/id #uuid "f9c3ead6-13a5-5467-b263-692993144dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-1/7be955a0d30e160f7085e4965e8dbd5ddbf08145c95295dc1c1ff167270037f7" :answer/id #uuid "59d2be2b-9f49-551c-8278-3004b07dd9a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-1/800dbffbdda5ce97831ade7e5e6c9f6e5e28b6d2149e4a3ae6905f42949ec499" :answer/id #uuid "5bf73802-f17e-5f1f-b851-1a013e4823dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "09b00a5e-6dcb-55a4-b145-5a064f173be4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339867/field-1/9249ec38b82e800720a79ba1072f7d45fcdfddce5a4914d085b8568b9b083f3a" :answer-field/choices ["q-339867/field-1/9249ec38b82e800720a79ba1072f7d45fcdfddce5a4914d085b8568b9b083f3a" "q-339867/field-1/7be955a0d30e160f7085e4965e8dbd5ddbf08145c95295dc1c1ff167270037f7" "q-339867/field-1/800dbffbdda5ce97831ade7e5e6c9f6e5e28b6d2149e4a3ae6905f42949ec499"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-2/01589de194bbd213963beb2c2b23df190d03dcb2ab541aaa83f960eedb5ab71f" :answer/id #uuid "95560d05-23a8-56ee-ab73-36da539c3aba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.92" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-2/6940c12264ef122fb28457ea192ebda6abde48cc366c9a909cf0d8462e9bdd5e" :answer/id #uuid "a7e73c72-5fc3-50cf-a302-94c23b01d8c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.08" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-2/45eef6f49b0390362a23da95e1007ab57f716f098ec9bfc15f15cb0ce116c4c3" :answer/id #uuid "49a446ee-b4cf-5c52-9536-de45c80824d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.99" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f48a4bb-1a29-5c6f-98bd-e8c85131a21e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339867/field-2/6940c12264ef122fb28457ea192ebda6abde48cc366c9a909cf0d8462e9bdd5e" :answer-field/choices ["q-339867/field-2/01589de194bbd213963beb2c2b23df190d03dcb2ab541aaa83f960eedb5ab71f" "q-339867/field-2/6940c12264ef122fb28457ea192ebda6abde48cc366c9a909cf0d8462e9bdd5e" "q-339867/field-2/45eef6f49b0390362a23da95e1007ab57f716f098ec9bfc15f15cb0ce116c4c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339867" :question/id #uuid "db2f949b-750b-515e-9876-2557de248bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339867" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =1.30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+3\\cdot (1.30)) \\\\  & =1.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.08.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339867/field-1" "q-339867/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339867"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872/field-1/e83d81bf0e746542edf4f1eda1ad3a719b4408646c130801bab8c838604ed91b" :answer/id #uuid "ab1ecad0-c508-54da-844a-502d728b3933" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872/field-1/d0093d0b299790fb66e2264825c8fd36d46a8a0cf34c8c97bfba1ca28051d69a" :answer/id #uuid "85da2710-5f0c-5639-9a12-0f6374fc441a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872/field-1/ec2db8747e7c42b612af95552e9d0366957758c0e265e5507c2f04b1ec7613e6" :answer/id #uuid "92e32ff7-280d-5e3f-8e02-15c1687321da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872/field-1/0a4d6c1d40cc39b940e2e777304a90dfd4a8e6958e913de83f57d12051966f08" :answer/id #uuid "cc2c3dd1-f40b-5619-a10d-bf5c1655ce14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "238c380e-4d09-51f3-bd9b-0be5a7c8f355" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339872/field-1/e83d81bf0e746542edf4f1eda1ad3a719b4408646c130801bab8c838604ed91b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339872/field-1/e83d81bf0e746542edf4f1eda1ad3a719b4408646c130801bab8c838604ed91b" "q-339872/field-1/d0093d0b299790fb66e2264825c8fd36d46a8a0cf34c8c97bfba1ca28051d69a" "q-339872/field-1/ec2db8747e7c42b612af95552e9d0366957758c0e265e5507c2f04b1ec7613e6" "q-339872/field-1/0a4d6c1d40cc39b940e2e777304a90dfd4a8e6958e913de83f57d12051966f08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339872" :question/id #uuid "6fa7504e-729d-57d8-a90a-a74d6bb6f493" :question/math-academy-id "q-339872" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{x}^{3}+3),\\,y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2{x}^{3}+3)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+7)}{1-2\\cdot {0}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & ≈2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈2.25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339872/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339872"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
